stural Hypotension vs Inherited Long QT Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  abnormal autonomic nervous system physiology, autosomal recessive inheritance, chest pain, confusion, decreased glomerular filtration rate\n  Favours second: abnormality of the ear, autosomal dominant inheritance, bradycardia, cardiac arrest, congestive heart failure\n\nOrthostatic/Postural Hypotension vs Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):\n  Favours first:  abnormal autonomic nervous system physiology, blurred vision, confusion, decreased glomerular filtration rate, dizziness\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nOndansetron-Induced Torsades De Pointes vs Inherited Long QT Syndrome:\n  Favours second: abnormality of the ear, autosomal dominant inheritance, blurred vision, bradycardia, cardiac arrest\n\nOndansetron-Induced Torsades De Pointes vs Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):\n  Favours first:  ventricular arrhythmia\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nInherited Long QT Syndrome vs Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the ear, blurred vision, bradycardia, cardiac arrest, congestive heart failure\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nNOT typically seen in Inherited Long QT Syndrome: hearing abnormality\n\nRelated to Orthostatic/Postural Hypotension: dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency, genetic nervous system disorder, hereditary late onset parkinson disease\n\nRelated to Ondansetron-Induced Torsades De Pointes: ventricular tachycardia\n\nRelated to Inherited Long QT Syndrome: catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, congenital heart disease, familial long qt syndrome\n\nRelated to Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI): hemoglobinopathy\n\n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.62):]\n  Source: Consensus statement on the definition of orthostatic hypotension, neurally mediated syncope and the postural tachycardia syndrome. > Introduction\n  Syncope is a common presenting complaint. It refers to a transient or passing loss of consciousness resulting from a reduction in cerebral blood flow and is usually associated with a loss in postural \n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.63):]\n  Source: Hypomagnesemia and hypermagnesemia. > History and Physical\n  Around 50% of patients with Torsades de Pointes are asymptomatic. The most common symptoms reported are syncope, palpitations, and dizziness. However, cardiac death is the presenting symptom in up to \n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Hypomagnesemia and hypermagnesemia. > History and Physical\n  Around 50% of patients with Torsades de Pointes are asymptomatic. The most common symptoms reported are syncope, palpitations, and dizziness. However, cardiac death is the presenting symptom in up to \n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.79):]\n  Source: Neurology_Adams\n  had orthostatic hypotension. One of the patients died in his twelfth year and was found to have an absence of small neurons in the dorsal root ganglia, an absence of Lissauer tracts, and a decrease in\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  fainted after standing up suddenly in the heat → vasovagal syncope → Acute Vagal/Neurological Syncope with ECG Artifact\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Assess whether the syncope was triggered by a vagal reflex during sudden orthostasis in hot conditions, potentially explaining the acute neurological etiology"}
