sed ECG abnormality (Tier-1 priority)", "Recent unregulated medication use"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Orthostatic/Postural Hypotension", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.20908660639330615, "posterior": 0.256979990446367, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Preserved supine blood pressure and no positional history argue against orthostatic hypotension as the fainting mechanism, but the LR+ of 1.6142 indicates only minor support, aligning with its current moderate elevation.", "Rapid neurological recovery weakly favors orthostatic/postural syncope over more dangerous arrhythmic causes, as brain injury is unlikely."], "evidence_against": ["The absence of orthostatic pressure changes with preserved baseline BP moderately supports alternative causes over orthostatic hypotension given the lack of positional syncope features in this patient’s presentation.", "Unknown positional BP data moderately reduces support for orthostatic hypotension (B1) despite environmental risk factors for volume depletion."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of positional changes leading to syncope", "Presence of postural symptoms in past medical history"], "requestable_discriminators": ["ECG for signs of sinoatrial node dysfunction", "Orthostatic blood pressure measurements"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Cardiac Arrhythmia with Torsades-Des-Pointes Potential", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.16428233359474056, "posterior": 0.1560235656281514, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Rapid recovery makes torsades de pointes less likely, but the ECG remains the definitive test for this condition.", "No known CAD and lack of described ECG changes for STEMI argue against acute MI as the primary diagnosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Medication history (including off-label use) linked to QT prolongation", "Witnessed arrhythmic features on this ECG"], "requestable_discriminators": ["ECG for QT interval and T-wave morphology", "Serum potassium and magnesium levels"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.7, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism"}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Cardiac Ischemia (Acute/Previous)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.10422892361267055, "posterior": 0.08565999681545566, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["No known CAD and lack of described ECG changes for STEMI argue against acute MI as the primary diagnosis."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Description of chest pain precipitating this episode"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Ischemic ECG changes (ST elevation/depression)", "Cardiac biomarkers (troponin)"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 3.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.5, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Acute Vagal/Neurological Syncope with ECG Artifact", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.17131999363091133, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B2.1": {"id": "B2.1", "label": "Ondansetron-Induced Torsades De Pointes", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.04588928400827982, "danger": 0.8, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Uncharacterised ECG findings without hypokalemic patterns significantly reduce risk for drug-induced torsades (B2.1), which requires explicit QT interval and T-wave analysis to confirm."]}, "B2.2": {"id": "B2.2", "label": "Hypokalemic-Induced Torsades", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.04894856960883181, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of hypokalemia-specific ECG features provides weak evidence against the hypokalemic torsades hypothesis, though electrolyte confirmation remains necessary for final assessment."]}, "B2.3": {"id": "B2.3", "label": "Inherited Long QT Syndrome", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.06118571201103977, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B2", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Absence of family history for sudden cardiac events moderately argues against inherited LQTS (B2.3) as a cause, though this remains a parked diagnosis unless specific genotype or ECG features emerge."]}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Previous Myocardial Infarction with Pathologic Q-Waves", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.04244360702722786, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["While lacking chest pain history is suggestive, definitive evidence against prior MI (B3.1) requires explicit absence of Q-waves. Current findings only moderately support excluding MI."]}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI)", "level": 2, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.030019638023459482, "danger": 0.75, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of CAD history and no chest pain reported provide moderate evidence against subacute ischemia (B3.2) and transient coronary obstruction (B3.3) as causes of this syncope."]}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Transient Coronary Obstruction / Valsalva-Induced Ischemia", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.013196751764768325, "danger": 0.65, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Lack of CAD history and no chest pain reported provide moderate evidence against subacute ischemia (B3.2) and transient coronary obstruction (B3.3) as causes of this syncope."]}}, "frontier": ["B1"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the rapid neurological recovery with intact cranial nerves and gait (suggesting no brain injury) provide modest supportive evidence for orthostatic/postural causes of syncope?", "result_summary": "Rapid neurological recovery weakly favors orthostatic/postural syncope over more dangerous arrhythmic causes, as brain injury is unlikely.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the ECG in Figure A provide pathognomonic findings (QTc interval >500ms, torsades morphology) required to safely exclude ondansetron-induced torsades des pointes as a high-risk alternative?", "result_summary": "Absence of torsades-specific ECG findings (QTc >500ms, characteristic morphology) provides strong evidence against drug-induced torsades (B2.1) in this case.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the ECG in Figure A demonstrate pathologic Q-waves or T-wave changes indicative of chronic myocardial infarction, which would confirm prior MI as a causitive mechanism?", "result_summary": "Lack of pathologic Q-waves and T-wave abnormalities on ECG provides strong evidence against previous MI (B3.1) as a structural explanation for syncope.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B3.1": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented orthostatic BP measurements or positional history (despite hot weather exposure) moderate against orthostatic/postural hypotension as the primary mechanism for fainting?", "result_summary": "Unknown positional BP data moderately reduces support for orthostatic hypotension (B1) despite environmental risk factors for volume depletion.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of hypokalemic ECG features (U-waves, flattened T-waves) and explicit mention of uncharacterised ECG findings provide strong evidence against ondansetron-induced torsades as the primary etiology?", "result_summary": "Uncharacterised ECG findings without hypokalemic patterns significantly reduce risk for drug-induced torsades (B2.1), which requires explicit QT interval and T-wave analysis to confirm.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B2.1": "strong_against"}}, {"timestep": 4, "bundle_id": 4, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Given no chest pain history and absence of Q-waves in ECG description, does this provide definitive evidence against prior myocardial infarction as the source of the current syncopal episode?", "result_summary": "While lacking chest pain history is suggestive, definitive evidence against prior MI (B3.1) requires explicit absence of Q-waves. Current findings only moderately support excluding MI.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B3.1": "moderate_against"}}], "differential_history": [{"B1": 0.30776349980422163, "B2": 0.20517566653614777, "B3": 0.07670950058733492, "B4": 0.10258783326807389, "B2.1": 0.10991553564436486, "B2.2": 0.05862161901032794, "B2.3": 0.03663851188145496, "B3.1": 0.05083116790659594, "B3.2": 0.017976008258284982, "B3.3": 0.00790232442245399}, {"B1": 0.20908660639330615, "B2": 0.16428233359474056, "B3": 0.10422892361267055, "B4": 0.13939107092887076, "B2.1": 0.07467378799760933, "B2.2": 0.03982602026539165, "B2.3": 0.04978252533173957, "B3.1": 0.06906677629647032, "B3.2": 0.02442487537095064, "B3.3": 0.010737271945249587}, {"B1": 0.256979990446367, "B2": 0.1560235656281514, "B3": 0.08565999681545566, "B4": 0.17131999363091133, "B2.1": 0.04588928400827982, "B2.2": 0.04894856960883181, "B2.3": 0.06118571201103977, "B3.1": 0.04244360702722786, "B3.2": 0.030019638023459482, "B3.3": 0.013196751764768325}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Recent unregulated ondansetron use (QT prolonger) combined with uncharacterised ECG abnormality creates immediate suspicion for torsades des pointes. QT interval analysis and electrolyte checks are critically needed to confirm/deny this time-sensitive arrhythmia.", "required_actions": ["Analyze ECG for QTc interval prolongation and torsades morphology", "Order serum potassium and magnesium to evaluate for contributory electrolyte disturbance"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "None of the six termination conditions apply. B1 posterior (0.257) <0.75 and no single actionable parent branch exists. Dangerous alternatives B2.1 (d=0.8) and B3.2 (d=0.75) with unresolved ECG and electrolyte data remain. Required ECG analysis and electrolyte confirmation tests are pending."}, "turn_budget_used": 4, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.0, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.256979990446367, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_vignette": "A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting. He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat, when he stood up suddenly from pulling weeds and fainted. He denies any preceding symptoms and returned to baseline within 1 minute. The patient is not aware of any medical problems and just started seeing a primary care physician last week. He recently used a friend's prescription for ondansetron for nausea. His temperature is 99.3°F (37.4°C), blood pressure is 142/88 mmHg, pulse is 107/min, respirations are 14/min, and oxygen saturation is 99% on room air. Physical exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal strength and sensation, and a stable gait. His abdomen is soft and nontender. An ECG is performed as seen in Figure A.", "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis based on this patient’s ECG?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute myocardial infarction"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Hypokalemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Intermittent torsades des pointes"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Previous myocardial ischemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Pulmonary embolism"}], "static_evidence_items": [{"id": "E1", "kind": "direct", "content": "Faints suddenly after standing up from weeding in hot weather", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E2", "kind": "direct", "content": "Denies preceding symptoms, recovers within 1 minute", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E3", "kind": "direct", "content": "Unfamiliar with medical problems, only recently began primary care follow-up", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E4", "kind": "direct", "content": "Recent use of ondansetron via friend's prescription", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E5", "kind": "direct", "content": "Vitals: temp 99.3°F, BP 142/88, pulse 107, respirations 14, O2 sat 99%", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E6", "kind": "direct", "content": "Physical exam: intact cranial nerves, normal strength/sensation, stable gait, soft/nontender abdomen", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}, {"id": "E7", "kind": "image_reference", "content": "ECG shown in Figure A — image not available in text form", "source_ids": [], "independent": true, "branch_links": {}, "metadata": {}}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Acute coronary syndrome', 'myocardial infarction', 'anger', 't-wave inversion', 'Myocardial infarction', 'Abnormal coronary artery physiology'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=60%, source=both]\n\nOrthostatic/Postural Hypotension vs Inherited Long QT Syndrome:\n  Favours first:  abnormal autonomic nervous system physiology, autosomal recessive inheritance, chest pain, confusion, decreased glomerular filtration rate\n  Favours second: abnormality of the ear, autosomal dominant inheritance, bradycardia, cardiac arrest, congestive heart failure\n\nOrthostatic/Postural Hypotension vs Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):\n  Favours first:  abnormal autonomic nervous system physiology, blurred vision, confusion, decreased glomerular filtration rate, dizziness\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nInherited Long QT Syndrome vs Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the ear, blurred vision, bradycardia, cardiac arrest, congestive heart failure\n  Favours second: 2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abdominal pain, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity\n\nNOT typically seen in Inherited Long QT Syndrome: hearing abnormality\n\nRelated to Orthostatic/Postural Hypotension: dopamine beta-hydroxylase deficiency, genetic nervous system disorder, hereditary late onset parkinson disease\n\nRelated to Inherited Long QT Syndrome: catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, congenital heart disease, familial long qt syndrome\n\nRelated to Subacute Ischemia / Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction (NSTEMI): hemoglobinopathy\n\n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.64):]\n  Source: [Current practice for the prevention of sudden death in young athletes]. > Evaluation\n  Long QT syndrome is a known cause of syncope and sudden death among the general population and athletes. The most common cause of prolonged QT on an ECG is secondary to medication use.[24] The absence\n[RAG context for 'A 67-year-old man presents to the emergency department after fainting' (score=0.67):]\n  Source: Neurology_Adams\n  Tachyarrhythmias alone are less likely to produce syncope. Certainly, intermittent ventricular fibrillation can cause fainting, and supraventricular tachycardias with rapid ventricular responses (usua\n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.70):]\n  Source: Hypomagnesemia and hypermagnesemia. > Etiology\n  Two rare congenital long QT syndromes include Romano-Ward syndrome and Jervell and Lange Nielsen syndrome.\n[RAG context for 'He was outside tending to his lawn for several hours in the heat' (score=0.74):]\n  Source: Investigating the Complex Arrhythmic Phenotype Caused by the Gain-of-Function Mutation KCNQ1-G229D. > History and Physical\n  Syncope is the most common symptom, usually experienced during exercise and high emotions (50% of the genetic variant). Syncope during swimming, including immediately after diving into the water, appe\n[Indirect reasoning chains (clinical inference):]\n  fainted after standing up suddenly from pulling weeds in the heat → orthostatic hypotension due to dehydration → Orthostatic/Postural Hypotension\n    (frequency: frequent)\n    Suggestion: Investigate orthostatic blood pressure changes and consider dehydration as an exacerbating factor for postural hypotension\n  recent use of ondansetron from a friend → drug-induced QT prolongation → Inherited Long QT Syndrome\n    (frequency: rare (ondansetron is a moderate QT prolonger))\n    Suggestion: Review ECG for QT interval and consider family history of syncope/sudden death to confirm LQTS\n\nWhen indirect_reasoning_chains are present, generate candidates that investigate the INTERMEDIATE phenotype to confirm or rule out the indirect association."}
